<% content_for :title, "#{@project.name} — Tinkerex" %>

<% content_for :crumbs do %>
  <nav class="breadcrumb" aria-label="Project path">
    <%= link_to "Projects", projects_path %>
    <span class="sep" aria-hidden="true">/</span>
    <span aria-current="page"><%= @project.name %></span>
  </nav>
<% end %>

<%#
  THE HUB'S HEAD (structural redesign §6, slice 6): the cover, a PROJECT eyebrow, the name, then the
  badges and links that were always here. STILL TWO SLOTS — `.page-title` and `.head-actions` — which
  is what `.page-head` lays out (template_hygiene_test.rb); the cover and the text column are both
  inside `.page-title`. The eyebrow sits OUTSIDE the h1, whose exact text a test pins.

  The hero partial is unchanged and still decides whether there is a cover at all (no cover renders
  nothing, so the head is the text column alone); pages/projects.css places it on the photo stage
  and sizes it for the head. The text column is its own div because a cover beside a wrapping row
  of name, badges and links is two boxes, not one flex row.
%>
<div class="page-head project-head">
  <div class="page-title">
    <%= render "hero" %>
    <div class="project-head-text">
      <span class="eyebrow">Project</span>
      <h1><%= @project.name %></h1>
      <% if @project.status == "archived" %>
        <span class="badge status-archived">Archived</span>
      <% end %>
      <%#
        THE STATUS, WHERE IT CAN BE SEEN (stage 4, S4-6; story 1.6). The form can set it again, and a
        value nobody can see is a field nobody will use. `planning` is the column default and badging a
        default is noise — the same argument S4-1's quiet panels make about a zero. `archived` keeps its
        own badge above; this covers active, paused and done.
      %>
      <% if @project.status.in?(%w[active paused done]) %>
        <span class="badge status-<%= @project.status %>"><%= @project.status.humanize %></span>
      <% end %>
      <% if @project.published? %>
        <span class="badge visibility-<%= @project.visibility %>"><%= @project.visibility.humanize %></span>
        <%# An unlisted project's link carries ?k=<token>, which IS the credential for that page --
            a public one needs none. %>
        <%= link_to "Public page",
                    public_project_path(@project.user.handle, @project.slug,
                                        **(@project.unlisted? ? { k: @project.unlisted_token } : {})),
                    class: "linkbtn small public-link" %>
      <% end %>
      <%# THE DOOR TO THE PANEL (stage 4, S4-4). A plain in-page anchor: the panel is the LAST panel
          so it does not displace the maker's own content, and the head is where a maker already
          looks for publishing state. %>
      <%= link_to (@project.published? ? "Publishing" : "Publish"), "#publish", class: "linkbtn small publish-link" %>
    </div>
  </div>
  <%# ARCHIVE'S OWN CONFIRM SAYS THE UNDOCUMENTED HALF (stage 4, S4-6; story 1.6): for a project
      the app adopted, archiving is also what stops it being adopted again. A local rather than a
      helper — one call site, and the sentence belongs at the point of the click. %>
  <% archive_confirm = if @project.discovered_at.present?
       "Archive #{@project.name}? Its folder stays in your library, and the app won't adopt it as a project again."
     else
       "Archive #{@project.name}?"
     end %>
  <div class="head-actions">
    <%= link_to "Edit", edit_project_path(@project), class: "btn small" %>
    <%# THE PAGE EDITOR (2026-09-09): the same page with the chrome on. A link, because entering
        edit mode changes nothing until Save. %>
    <%= link_to "Customise page", edit_layout_project_path(@project), class: "btn small customise-link" %>
    <% if Array(@absent_panels).any? %>
      <%#
        "+ ADD PANEL" (structural redesign §6, slice 6): the on-demand panels absent because they are
        empty (ProjectsController#show's @absent_panels, never set in the editor). Each link opens its
        panel for this visit (?add=), empty and with its composer, and lands on the panel's own anchor
        (ProjectsHelper::PANEL_ANCHORS); it stays on its own once it holds a row. Nothing is saved.
        Labelled as the panel is headed, so a maker's own title reads the same in both places.
      %>
      <details class="menu add-panel" data-controller="menu">
        <summary class="btn small">+ Add panel</summary>
        <div class="menu-list">
          <% @absent_panels.each do |key| %>
            <%= link_to panel_title(@panel_layout, key),
                        project_path(@project, add: key, anchor: ProjectsHelper::PANEL_ANCHORS.fetch(key)),
                        class: "menu-item" %>
          <% end %>
        </div>
      </details>
    <% end %>
    <%#
      THE ⋯ MENU (structural redesign §6, slice 6): the occasional acts, out of the head's one row.
      Publish settings is the same in-page anchor the head's Publish link is; Archive, Unarchive and
      Not a project moved here VERBATIM — the same button_to, routes and confirms, with menu-item for
      the button class (Archive keeps `danger`). Nothing in here is an accent: the hub's two are the
      top bar's Upload and the Publish panel's Save publishing (design-system.md rule 13).
    %>
    <details class="menu project-menu" data-controller="menu">
      <summary class="btn small icon" aria-label="More actions" title="More actions">⋯</summary>
      <div class="menu-list">
        <%= link_to "Publish settings", "#publish", class: "menu-item" %>
        <% if @project.status == "archived" %>
          <%= button_to "Unarchive", unarchive_project_path(@project), method: :patch, class: "menu-item" %>
        <% else %>
          <% if @project.discovered_at.present? %>
            <%#
              NOT A PROJECT (stage 4, S4-6; story 1.6). The app made this row from a folder, so the
              maker needs a way to say it was wrong — and archiving already IS that mechanism, silently:
              the claim lookup is unscoped, so an archived project keeps its folder_node_id and the
              sweep passes over that folder forever. This names the act instead of leaving it to be
              discovered. ONE WRITE, TWO SENTENCES — see ProjectsController#destroy; a second route
              would be two ways to do one thing.

              Only for a discovered project: a project you made by hand was never adopted, so there is
              nothing for "not a project" to mean.
            %>
            <%= button_to "Not a project", project_path(@project, dismiss: 1), method: :delete,
                          class: "menu-item",
                          form: { data: { turbo_confirm: "“#{@project.name}” is not a project? Its folder stays in your library, and the app won't adopt it again." } } %>
          <% end %>
          <%= button_to "Archive", project_path(@project), method: :delete, class: "menu-item danger",
                        form: { data: { turbo_confirm: archive_confirm } } %>
        <% end %>
      </div>
    </details>
  </div>
</div>

<%= render "projects/move_conflict" %>

<% if @project.description.present? %>
  <p class="project-description"><%= @project.description %></p>
<% end %>
<%= tag_chips(@project) %>

<%# THE STATS LINE reads collections ProjectsController#show already loaded — @listing, @tasks,
    @entries, @sheets, @linked_rows — and nothing else, so page_query_budget_test.rb's "does not
    grow with attachments, tasks or build-log entries" holds without a counter cache. The file
    count is the FOLDER's (@listing.total), not maker_file_attachments — an attachment to a node
    living elsewhere in the library (Linked) is counted separately, right here, rather than folded
    into the first number. %>
<% done = @tasks.count { |t| t.status == "done" } %>
<p class="project-stats">
  <span><b><%= @listing.total %></b> <%= "file".pluralize(@listing.total) %></span>
  <% if @linked_rows.any? %>
    <span><b><%= @linked_rows.size %></b> linked</span>
  <% end %>
  <span><b><%= done %> of <%= @tasks.size %></b> <%= "task".pluralize(@tasks.size) %> done</span>
  <span><b><%= @entries.size %></b> log <%= "entry".pluralize(@entries.size) %></span>
  <span><b><%= @sheets.size %></b> <%= "sheet".pluralize(@sheets.size) %></span>
</p>

<%#
  THE PANELS, AS DATA (stage 4, S4-7; story 6.5). Ten hard-coded renders became one loop over
  Maker::ProjectKind::Layout#visible — this project's kind's preset with its own overrides on top.
  The key IS the partial's basename, and Maker::Project::PANELS is the only place a FIXED key may
  come from: Maker::ProjectKind.layout_for intersects the stored column with it and
  Projects::PanelsController intersects every write, so this render can never be handed a partial
  name that does not exist.

  A KEY IS EITHER FIXED OR A FOLDER (2026-09-08, folder panels; spec decisions 3 and 5). A
  `folder:<node_id>` key renders projects/_folder_panel with the Dir it names — one of the
  project folder's own DEPTH-ONE subfolders, which is why Files no longer renders a section per
  subfolder itself. The interpolated render is reached only by the fixed branch, so widening the
  vocabulary did not widen what `render "projects/#{key}"` can be handed.

  THE `find` IS IN MEMORY over at most DIR_CAP dirs and CANNOT ANSWER NIL: the layout's folder
  keys were built from `@listing.panel_folders`, i.e. from this same `@listing.tree`, one
  statement earlier in ProjectsController#show. A `dir:` guard here would be a branch for a state
  no request can reach, and it would hide the real bug (a layout built from a different listing)
  behind a silently missing panel.

  HIDDEN IS NOT EMPTY. A panel the maker hid is absent and stays absent. An EMPTY on-demand panel
  (ProjectsController::ON_DEMAND) is absent from this page too since slice 6, but the head's
  "+ Add panel" offers it, and one opened with ?add= renders quiet (S4-1's panel--quiet) — its
  heading and one add control — as an empty folder panel still does. Two questions, and the
  overview's quiet-panel contract is what keeps them two. The editor shows every panel.

  THE LOCALS MAP EXISTS BECAUSE THREE PARTIALS TAKE LOCALS AND SEVEN READ IVARS, and neither group
  may be converted: projects/_wiring, projects/_releases and projects/_publish take locals so the
  PUBLIC page can render the same markup without aliasing its own ivars to this page's names --
  each of those partials says so in its own header. Every value here is an ivar
  ProjectsController#show has already loaded, so building the map costs nothing, including for a
  panel this loop is about to skip.

  THE SLOT (2026-09-09): see projects/_panel_slot.html.erb.
%>
<% panel_locals = {
     "wiring"   => { project: @project, connections: @connections, editable: true, layout: @panel_layout },
     "releases" => { project: @project, releases: @releases, current_tokens: @release_tokens,
                     repos: @release_repos, editable: true },
     "publish"  => { project: @project, folder_path: (@listing.path if @listing&.available?) }
   } %>
<%#
  EVERY PANEL RENDERS INSIDE A SLOT (2026-09-09, the page editor; spec §3 and §5): projects/
  _panel_slot wraps the panel in `div.panel-slot.panel--<width>` on the normal page, and in edit
  mode adds the strip and the data attributes the layout_editor controller reads its draft from.
  The width used to be a stylesheet rule naming three panel classes; it is the layout's answer now.

  IN EDIT MODE THE LOOP IS `order`, NOT `visible`: a hidden panel renders ghosted in its place so
  it can be dragged and shown again. Its data was not loaded (the controller skips a hidden
  panel's queries), so it renders quiet — which is the honest ghost.
%>
<%#
  THE GRID PACKS ON WIDE SCREENS (2026-09-09, content blocks; spec §7). `masonry` is on ALL THREE
  renders — this page, edit mode, and the visitor's — because a gap under a short half-width panel
  is the same gap wherever it is drawn. Below the two-column breakpoint, and with no JavaScript at
  all, the grid is exactly today's: the controller measures nothing and adds no class.
%>
<% if @editing %>
  <div class="layout-editor" data-controller="layout-editor"
       data-layout-editor-yaml-url-value="<%= yaml_project_layout_path(@project) %>"
       data-layout-editor-parse-url-value="<%= parse_project_layout_path(@project) %>"
       data-layout-editor-panel-url-value="<%= layout_panel_project_path(@project) %>"
       data-layout-editor-preview-url-value="<%= preview_project_layout_path(@project) %>"
       data-layout-editor-block-cap-value="<%= Maker::ProjectBlock::CAP %>"
       data-layout-editor-tab-cap-value="<%= Maker::ProjectKind::TAB_CAP %>">
    <%= render "projects/layout_bar" %>
    <%= render "projects/page_intro" %>
    <div class="project-panels layout-editing" data-controller="masonry" data-layout-editor-target="grid">
      <% @panel_layout.order.each do |key| %>
        <%= render "projects/panel_slot", key:, extra: panel_locals.fetch(key, {}) %>
      <% end %>
    </div>
    <%= render "projects/layout_drawer" %>
    <%= render "projects/block_templates" %>
  </div>
<% else %>
  <%# THE GROUPED SHAPE (2026-09-09, tabs; spec §6): projects/_panel_groups renders today's one grid
      when the layout has no tab, and the top group, the bar, the panes and Publish when it has.
      MINUS THE ABSENT ON-DEMAND PANELS (slice 6): the keys are narrowed HERE, not in the layout the
      public page shares, so an empty Build log, Tasks, BOM, Wiring, Releases or Sheets is never
      yielded at all. A tab left holding none of its keys drops out with its bar link, because
      _panel_groups renders only the panes something rendered into. %>
  <%= render layout: "projects/panel_groups",
             locals: { groups: @panel_layout.groups(@panel_layout.visible - Array(@absent_panels)), tail: nil } do |key| %>
    <%= render "projects/panel_slot", key: key, extra: panel_locals.fetch(key, {}) %>
  <% end %>
<% end %>
